 "notes": "The list is on two pages: p. 79 (n94.jpg, Nos. 115–132) and p. 80 (n95.jpg, Nos. 133–167). n93.jpg (p. 78) and n96.jpg (p. 81) hold none of the list. The OCR's lines 4594–4650 cover p. 79 and lines 4652–4828 cover p. 80.\n\nChecks against the lead-in: the list has 53 stations (Nos. 115–167, with no gaps) and 39 are marked with an asterisk. Both numbers match the lead-in's \"fifty-three\" and \"thirty-nine\". The asterisk is printed before the number (e.g. \"*117.\"), so the number field holds the bare number and the asterisk goes in marks. \"L.H.\" and \"L.V.\" are printed in small capitals. Dot leaders are shortened to \"......\" in printed, because their length is only typesetting. On p. 79, \"Messrs Prichard, Roberts,\" is set with little or no space between the words. I have used normal spacing.\n\nThe region labels are printed sideways beside braces: \"Isle of Man.\" (115–119), \"N.-W. England.\" (120–123), \"North Wales.\" (124–132), \"South Wales.\" (133–138), \"Bristol Channel.\" (139–146) and \"South-Western England.\" (147–167). There is no character, height or other column.\n\nSpellings copied as printed, not corrected:\n- \"Air\" (No. 124) is Point of Air/Ayr.\n- \"Caldy\" is Caldey.\n- \"Chickens Rock\" has no apostrophe.\n- \"Flatholm\" is printed that way.\n- \"Carnarvon Bay\" is printed that way.\n- \"Nash (E.)\" means East Nash.\n- \"Milford (St Ann's)\" is printed with the place name in parentheses. I kept the whole thing as the name.\n- \"Havells\" (Scarweather) may be a misprint for Howells, which appears at Helwick just above. It is printed \"Havells\".\n\nThe OCR misreads \"Portland Bill\" as \"Bull\". The image clearly says \"Bill\".\n\nParagraph on p. 81: the paragraph after the list thanks keepers by surname and station. Most agree with the list: Ainger, Baker, Clyne, Jones, Knott, Lewis, Nicholas, Rees (Skerries), Walters and Johnson, Webber and Howells, and Work and Clague. One does not: the paragraph says \"Johnson (Longships)\", but the list gives \"Jno. W. Watson\" for Longships (No. 153).\n\nKeeper entries with \"Messrs\" are kept as one entry each. Rows that give two names with initials or first names (\"Daniel Kneale and George Cumming\", \"S. Work and J. Clague\") are split into two people.",
